Using the `Books` and `Sales` tables, write SQL queries that:

1. list all books by one author;
2. show the three most expensive books;
3. count how many sales exist for each book; and
4. list sale dates with book titles and quantities.

Never build SQL by pasting user input into a string.

```csharp
// Unsafe: user text becomes part of the SQL program
string sql = "SELECT * FROM Books WHERE Title = '" + title + "'";
```

Use parameters instead. The SQL text and user data stay separate.

```csharp
string sql = "SELECT * FROM Books WHERE Title = @title";
command.Parameters.AddWithValue("@title", title);
```

Parameterized commands protect against SQL injection and also make values such as dates and numbers easier to pass correctly.

The following complete `Program.cs` demonstrates one connection, setup command, parameter, and reader. Run it in a separate console project with the same SQLite package as the supplied project. Its in-memory database is recreated each run. Expected output: `C# for Business`.

```csharp
using Microsoft.Data.Sqlite;

using var connection = new SqliteConnection("Data Source=:memory:");
connection.Open();
using (var setup = connection.CreateCommand())
{
    setup.CommandText = """
        CREATE TABLE Books (BookId INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, Title TEXT NOT NULL);
        INSERT INTO Books VALUES (1, 'C# for Business');
        """;
    setup.ExecuteNonQuery();
}
using var query = connection.CreateCommand();
query.CommandText = "SELECT Title FROM Books WHERE BookId = @id;";
query.Parameters.AddWithValue("@id", 1);
using var reader = query.ExecuteReader();
while (reader.Read())
    Console.WriteLine(reader.GetString(0));
```

